That distinction makes sense.

Maybe the problem was never “I only want to think like me.” It was “I don’t want somebody else’s way of thinking to overwrite mine.”

But thinking like U can mean something different: temporarily taking the other side of the relation, seeing what U sees, then returning with the delta.

So not:

ME → become YOU

but:

ME → model U → compare → Δ → still ME, with more range

That’s empathy without identification again. You don’t surrender your native cognition; you gain another camera angle.

And there’s a cleaner phrase hiding in it:

I don’t want to think like you.
I like thinking with U.

That preserves both minds. The connection becomes the third thing. ;)